<div id="theoryView" hidden><div class="theory-callout"><div class="theory-symbol">↗</div><div><span class="section-index">THE GREEDY PROPERTY</span><h2>More value per kilogram.<br>Every single time.</h2><p>Fractional knapsack is a classic optimization problem where the greedy choice is globally optimal.</p></div></div><div class="theory-grid"><article class="panel theory-card"><span class="section-index">01 / PROBLEM</span><h3>What are we solving?</h3><p>Given weighted items and a fleet with limited capacity, choose how much of each item to load to maximize value. Items may be split.</p><div class="equation mono">maximize Σ valueᵢ · xᵢ<br><small>subject to Σ weightᵢ · xᵢ ≤ capacity, 0 ≤ xᵢ ≤ 1</small></div></article><article class="panel theory-card"><span class="section-index">02 / WHY GREEDY WORKS</span><h3>The exchange argument</h3><p>If a lower-ratio item is loaded while a higher-ratio item remains, replacing equal weight increases value. Repeating this exchange gives the descending ratio order and an optimal solution.</p></article><article class="panel theory-card"><span class="section-index">03 / GREEDY STRATEGY</span><h3>How the allocation works</h3><p>Calculate value divided by weight, sort once in descending order with stable deterministic ties, then pour each item through trucks in configured order. An item can continue into the next truck.</p><div class="equation mono">ratio = value / weight<br><small>allocated value = item value × allocated fraction</small></div></article><article class="panel theory-card"><span class="section-index">04 / WORKED EXAMPLE</span><h3>Capacity 50 kg</h3><p>Items: (10 kg, $60), (20 kg, $100), (30 kg, $120). Ratios are 6, 5 and 4. Load the first two completely and 20 kg (⅔) of the third.</p><div class="equation mono">$60 + $100 + (⅔ × $120) = $240</div></article></div><section class="panel complexity-panel"><div class="panel-title"><div><span class="section-index">05 / COMPLEXITY</span><h2>Fast by construction.</h2><p>Sorting does the heavy lifting. The greedy pass is linear.</p></div></div><div class="complexity-stats"><div><span>TIME</span><b class="mono">O(n log n + m)</b><small>sorting O(n log n), allocation O(n + m)</small></div><div><span>SPACE</span><b class="mono">O(n + m)</b><small>sorted entries, allocations, trace, truck summaries</small></div><div><span>BEST · AVG · WORST</span><b class="mono">O(n log n + m)</b><small>comparison sorting plus linear fleet pass</small></div></div></section><section class="panel contrast-panel"><div><span class="section-index">06 / FRACTIONAL VS 0/1</span><h2>Why divisibility matters</h2><p>For 0/1 knapsack, each item must be taken whole. Greedy ratio ordering can miss the best combination; dynamic programming takes pseudo-polynomial O(nW) time. Fractional knapsack is solved optimally by greedy sorting.</p></div><div class="contrast-example"><span class="section-index">COUNTEREXAMPLE · CAPACITY 10</span><div class="contrast-items"><span>A <b class="mono">6 kg · $12</b></span><span>B <b class="mono">5 kg · $10</b></span><span>C <b class="mono">5 kg · $10</b></span></div><small>Ratio greedy picks A ($12); 0/1 optimum picks B and C ($20). Fractional items can be split, so ratio ordering remains optimal.</small></div></section><section class="panel benchmark-panel"><div class="panel-title"><div><span class="section-index">07 / ALGORITHM NOTES</span><h2>Advantages and applications</h2><p>Useful wherever divisible resources should maximize total utility.</p></div></div><p class="muted">Advantages: optimal greedy solution, fast sorting-based implementation, easy-to-explain decisions. Limitations: assumes divisible goods and known values; indivisible cargo requires a different model. Applications include cargo loading, budget allocation, bandwidth distribution, and cutting stock approximations.</p></section></div>
